  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 400 PM EDT THU OCT 29 2026\n\nThe extended forecast period opens Saturday morning with an\nexpansive upper-level ridge across the eastern CONUS and a digging\ntrough along the West Coast. At the surface, a weak boundary will be\ndraped near or just south of the area early Saturday morning before\nquickly lifting back northward across eastern Kentucky as a warm\nfront during the day. Low-level theta-e advection will allow\nSaturday afternoon temperatures to warm into the upper 60s to lower\n70s. Despite increasing cloud cover, pleasant conditions will\npersist for trick-or-treaters Saturday evening, with temperatures\ngradually cooling through the 60s after sunset and into the upper\n50s closer to midnight under light easterly winds. Temperatures look\nto cool further into the low to mid 50s by Sunday morning.\n\nMoisture will steadily advect into the Ohio Valley heading into\nSunday ahead of the developing Plains system, with PWATs climbing\ntoward 1.00 inch, or around the 80th to 85th climatological\npercentile. While rain chances will gradually increase from\nsouthwest to northeast through the day as isentropic ascent deepens\naloft, southeasterly downslope flow may dry out low levels across\nour eastern zones, which could result in a sharp QPF gradient. If\nthis occurs, measurable rainfall would remain light and favored\nprimarily across western portions of the forecast area, with many\neastern valleys receiving little to no accumulation. Both the 12Z\nNAM and GFS feature a sharp cutoff right over the forecast area,\nwhile the ECMWF remains even drier. Confidence in rainfall amounts\nremains low as a result. Furthermore, despite notable model spread\nregarding daytime temperatures, with an 8 to 10 degree difference\nbetween the 25th and 75th percentiles, southeasterly winds favoring\ndownslope warming off the higher terrain support the NBM solution of\nafternoon highs reaching the upper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nEastern Kentucky will remain embedded within the warm sector on\nMonday as broad southwesterly flow continues aloft ahead of the\ndeepening Plains system. With Sunday's lead impulse departing and\nthe primary trough still well to our west, lingering isolated to\nwidely scattered showers should wrap up by Monday afternoon. \nModest warm-air advection beneath persistent cloud cover will \nsupport Monday afternoon temperatures topping out near 70 \ndegrees, followed by a brief dry lull lasting through most of \nTuesday.\n\nRain chances ramp back up later Tuesday as deep Gulf moisture surges\nnorthward into the Ohio Valley ahead of the approaching surface cold\nfront. Continued warm-air advection will support unseasonably warm\nconditions, with afternoon highs reaching the mid to upper 70s on\nboth Tuesday and Wednesday (10 to 15 degrees above normal). While\nthe core of a potent 50 to 60 kt southerly Low-Level Jet remains\ndisplaced to our west initially - likely not crossing eastern \nKentucky until Wednesday evening - this strengthening low-level \nwind field should efficiently transport rich moisture into the \nregion well ahead of the boundary, potentially setting the stage \nfor a widespread soaking rain Wednesday into Thursday.",
